    "ls|LS16_S13_U_104": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(AYUSH)\nLOK  SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 104\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15TH DECEMBER, 2017\nMEDICINAL PLANTS\n104. SHRI K.C. VENUGOPAL:\nPROF. SAUGATA ROY:\nSHRI ARVIND SAWANT:\nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:\nSHRIMATI KAMLA DEVI PAATLE:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has conducted any survey to identify and document all\nmedicinal/aromatic plants in the country, if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to formulate a national policy of medicinal and aromatic\nplants in the country and if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the details of the steps taken by the Government for preservation, identification,\ndocumentation, cultivation and commercial use of such plants along with the funds approved,\nallocated and utilised for the purpose during each of the last three years;\n(d) whether the Government is aware that in many backward regions the tribals use these herbs\nas indigenous medicines for their treatment; and\n(e) if so, whether the Government proposes to open Medical Research Trainin g Centres in these\nbackward areas and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a): Yes, the Botanical Survey of India (BSI), an organization under Ministry of\nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change mandated for survey of plant diversity of the country,\nits documentation at National, Regional, State and Ecosystem level and it has been car rying out\nsurvey and documentation of all plant resources of the country including medicinal/aromatic\nplants.\nContd…………\n\n-2-\nAs per BSI, it is estimated that more than 8000 species of medicinal/aromatic plants are\nfound in the country.\n(b): Yes, Natio nal Medicinal Plants Board (NMPB), Ministry of AYUSH has initiated the\nwork related to formulation of a National Policy of Medicinal Plants. In this regard, the NMPB\nin collaboration with Federation of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants Stakeholders (FEDMAPS),\nNew Delhi had organized an 'International Symposium on Drafting a National Policy of\nMedicinal and Aromatic Plants of India' on 19 -20 January, 2017 at New Delhi. The outcome of\nsaid symposium was received as a draft structure of ‘National Policy Document o f Medicinal\nPlants’ and to further examine the draft structure of ‘National Policy Document of Medicinal\nPlants’, the NMPB had also organized one day review meeting on 22nd November 2017.\n(c): For conservation / preservation, identification and documenta tion, cultivation and\ncommercial use of medicinal plants in the country, presently the Ministry of AYUSH is\nimplementing the following schemes:\n(i) Central Sector Scheme on “Conservation, Development and Sustainable Management of\nMedicinal Plants” under w hich project based support is provided for survey, inventorization, in -\nsitu / ex -situ conservation, establishment of herbal gardens, linkage with Joint Forest\nManagement Committees (JFMCs) and  Research & Development etc.\n(ii) Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH Mission (NAM) under which the\n“Medicinal Plants” component is aimed at supporting large scale cultivation of medicinal plants\non private land and the financial assistance in this regard is provided as subsidy to farmers\nthroughout the cou ntry. Prior to NAM, the cultivation of medicinal plants has been supported\nunder Centrally Sponsored Scheme of “National Mission on Medicinal Plants” of NMPB\nimplemented between 2008-09 to 2014-15.\nThe funds allocated and expenditure incurred under the above schemes during each of the\nlast three years is at Annexure.\nIn addition, different research councils of Ministry of AYUSH viz. Central Council for\nResearch in Ayurvedic Science (CCRAS), Central Council for Research in Unani Medicine\n(CCRUM), Central Council for Research in Siddha (CCRS) and Central Council for Research in\nHomoeopathy (CCRH) are also involved in conservation / preservation, identification and\ndocumentation of medicinal plants. These research councils are main taining the germplasm of\nimportant medicinal plants in Herbal Gardens and carrying out identification and documentation\nand preservation of herbarium of medicinal plants.\n(d) & (e): Yes, the Government is aware that in many backward regions the tribals use herbs as\nindigenous medicines for their treatment and the research councils of Ministry of AYUSH are\ndocumenting ethno-medical claims from these areas.\nHowever, there is no such proposal under Ministry of AYUSH to open Medical Research\nTraining Centres.\n\nAnnexure\nThe funds allocated and expenditure incurred under Central Sector Scheme on\n“Conservation, Development and Sustainable Management of Medicinal Plants” and\nCentrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH Mission (NAM) and Centrally\nSponsored Scheme of “National Mission on Medicinal Plants” of NMPB during last three\nyears.\n(Rs. In lakh)\nName of the Scheme Year\nFunds\nAllocated\nExpenditure\nIncurred\nCentral Sector Scheme on\n“Conservation, Development and\nSustainable Management of Medicinal\nPlants”\n2014-15 6421.00 5811.26\n2015-16 6309.00 5294.98\n2016-17 6426.00 4931.84\nCentrally Sponsored Scheme of\nNational AYUSH Mission (NAM)\n2014-15* 4566.93 4566.93\n2015-16 4593.56 2779.83\n2016-17 7012.66 4287.09\n* The funds allocated and expenditure incurred under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of “National\nMission on Medicinal Plants”",
