    "ls|LS16_S13_U_73": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 73\nTO BE ANSWERED ON   15TH DECEMBER, 2017\nFUND ALLOCATION TO SPIP\n73. SHRI MANSUKHBHAI DHANJIBHAI VASAVA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has noticed that no funds have been given to the State of Gujarat\nunder State Programme Implementation Plan (SPIP) during the last two years;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and\n(c) the details of steps taken/being taken by the Government to equip all the hospitals of Gujarat\nwith modern technology?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a): The State of Gujarat has been given funds for execution of State Programme\nImplementation Plan (SPIP) under National Health Mission (NHM), flagship programme of the\nGovernment of India.  Details of the funds given during the last two years are at Annexure.\n(b): Question does not arise in view of answer at (a) above.\n(c): Public Health is a State subject. Under the NHM, financial and technical support is\nprovided to States/UTs including Gujarat to strengthen their healthcare system upto district\nhospital level including for physical infrastructure, equipment etc. based on the proposals\nreceived from respective States/UTs in their Programme Implementation Plan (PIPs).\n…………………\n\nANNEXURE\nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare\nNational Health Mission-Finance Division\nStatement showing the Release for the FY 2015-16 and 2016-17-Gujarat\n[Rs. In crore]\nSl. No. Programme 2015-16 2016-17\nRelease Release\nA.    NRHM-RCH\nFlexible Pool 411.00 410.89\n1 RCH Flexible Pool 192.34 192.40\n2 Mission Flexible Pool 196.32 194.85\n3 Routine Immunization 9.33 8.84\n4 Pulse Polio Immunisation 12.30 14.52\n5 National I.D.D. Control Prog. 0.71 0.28\nB.    National Urban\nHealth Mission-\nFlexible Pool\n48.98 43.31\nC. Flexible Pool for\nCommunicable\nDisease Control\nProgrammes\n50.33 56.45\na National Vector Borne\nDiseases Control Programme 11.60 10.79\nb Revised National Tuberculosis\nControl Prog.) 32.98 40.80\nc Natioinal Leprosy Eradication\nProg. 2.50 0.86\nd Integrated Disease Surveillance\nProject 3.25 4.00\nD. Flexible Pool for\nNon  Communicable\nDisease  Programmes\n20.98 21.32\nE. Infrastructure\nMaintenance 162.48 312.93\nGrand Total (A+B+C+D+E) 693.78 844.90\nNote:\nThe above release relates to Central Govt. Grants and does not include State share\ncontribution & Kind Grants under Immunisation.",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_74": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.74 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.12.2017 \n \nCoal Linkage and Supply to Thermal Power Plants \n \n*74. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether coal linkage and supply to various thermal power plants  in the country \nare being monitored by the Government and if so, the  details of the coal requirement \nraised by the plants against the  actual quantity supplied to the thermal power plants \nduring the last three years, State-wise; \n \n(b) whether adequate quantum o f coal is not being supplied as per  requirements of \nthe plants and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government has undertaken any study to ascertain  the primary \nreason for such shortage along with corrective measures  to be taken, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) whether the Government has evaluated the number of days that  the plants have \nbeen shut down as a result of non -availability of coal along with the loss incurred as \na result of such shut downs during  the said period and if so, the details thereof, \nplant-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF COAL IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL AND RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \nA statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo.74 for answer on 20.12.2017 asked by Shri Ravneet Singh. \n  \n(a) & (b): Based on the long -term Linkage granted by Standing Linkage Committee, \nFuel Supply Agreements are execu ted between Power Companies and Coal \nCompanies. Based on the Annual Contract Quantity, coal supply is made by the \nCoal Companies to the Power Stations.  In addition to the monitoring mechanism \navailable at coal companies and CIL, coal supplies to Power Uti lity sector is \nmonitored regularly by an inter -Ministerial Sub-Group comprising representatives of \nMinistry of Power, Ministry of Coal and Ministry of Railways constituted by the \nInfrastructure Review Committee of Cabinet Secretariat.  This Sub -Group takes \nvarious operational decisions for meeting any contingent situations relating to Power \nsector including critical coal stock position for power plants. Information regarding \ncoal supplied by CIL and Singareni Collieries Company Limited (SCCL) to the Power \nPlants against Annual Contracted Quantity state-wise for the last three years is being \ncollected and will be laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n(c): The Government has not undertaken any studies in this regard. However, \nCentral Electricity Authority has stipulated the normative coal stock to be maintained \nby every Thermal Power Plant. In the assessment of Ministry of Coal, there would be \nno shortage of coal for power generation if every power plant maintains the \nnormative coal stock as stipulated.   \n  \n(d): While Government has not done any evaluation of number of days that the \nplants have been shut down as a result of non-availability of coal, there has been 9% \ngrowth in coal dispatch by CIL during the period April to December, 2017 as \ncompared to despatch  during the same period of 2016 -17. This increased coal \nsupply has been mainly responsible for the 4.7% increase in coal based power \ngeneration. This increased domestic coal based power generation coexists with \n4.59% reduction in power generation by import ed coal based power plants of private \nsector. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_75": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 75\nTO BE ANSWERED ON   15TH DECEMBER, 2017\nWORLD BANK ASSISTANCE TO HOSPITALS\n75. SHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of World Bank projects for setting up of hospitals/ dispensaries in the country\nduring the last three years and the current year, State/UT-wise; and\n(b) the grant/assis tance received from the World Bank and the current status of implementation\nof the projects during the said period?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a) & (b):    The World Bank provides assistance in the form of loan to strengthen Central and\nState health systems.  As per information received from the Department of Economic Affairs, the\ndetails of the various projects under World Bank funding for health sector are at Annexure.\n……………….\n\nAnnexure\nLok Sabha Unstarred Question No.75 for 15-12-2017\nWorld Bank assisted Ongoing Health Sector Projects\nName of Projects State /\nCentral\nLoan\nAmount\nin US $\nmillion\nDate of Signing\nand Closing\nCumulative\nDisbursement as on\n12-12-2017\nIn US $ million\nNagaland Health Project Nagaland 48 16-01-2017 /\n31-03-2023\n1.33\nUttarakhand Health Systems\nDevelopment Project\nUttarakhand 100 23-03-2017 /\n30-09-2023\n0.045\nUttar Pradesh Health Systems\nStrengthening Project\nUttar Pradesh 152 21-03-2012 /\n30-03-20189\n76.57\nWorld Bank assisted Health Sector Projects Closed during the last three years\nName of Projects State /\nCentral\nLoan\nAmount\nin US $\nmillion\nDate of\nSigning and\nClosing\nProject Objective\nKarnataka Health Systerm\nDevelopment Project\n+ (Plus)\nAdditional Financing for\nKarnataka Health System\nDevelopment & Reform\nProject\nKarnataka 141.83\n70\n16-10-2006 /\n31-03-2016\n21-11-2012 /\n31-03-2017\nTo improve health service\ndelivery, public -private\ncollaboration, and financing,\nparticularly for the benefit of\nunderserved and vulnerable\ngroups in Karnataka.\nAdditional Financing for\nTamil Nadu Health Systems\nProject\nTamil\nNadu\n117 06-07-2010 /\n14-03-2016\nTo significantly improve the\neffectiveness of the health\nsystem, both public and private,\nin Tamil Nadu.\n^^^^^",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_76": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 76\nTO BE ANSWERED ON   15TH DECEMBER, 2017\nSETTING UP OF HERB BASED INDUSTRY\n76. SHRI LAXMAN GILUWA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has made any proposal to set up an industry based on herbs in\nSinghbhum district of Jharkhand; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a): No.\n(b): Does not arise.\n………………",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_77": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 77 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.12.2017 \n \nTHALASSERY-MYSORE RAIL LINE \n \n*77. SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN: \n  \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Governments of Kerala and Karnataka have entered \ninto any understanding/agreement regarding Thalassery -Mysore \nrailway line and if so,  the details thereof;   \n(b) whether the Railway Board has held any discussion with the \nGovernment of Kerala in Thiruv ananthapuram  recently in this \nregard; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Railway Board has deputed the Konkan Railway \nCorporation for the feasibility study of this line; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COAL \n \n (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (e):   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 77 BY SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.12.2017 REGARDING THALASSERY -\nMYSORE RAIL LINE \n \n(a):  Government of Kerala and Karnataka had joint meeting on \n09.11.2017 at Bengaluru wherein following decision was taken: \n“Permission to survey would depend upon the type of survey to \nbe conducted and therefore, Government of Kerala should send a \nproposal to Government of Karnataka. If it entails no disturbance \nof the wildlife areas, the Forest and Revenue Departments would \nassist Government of Kerala to give permission and conduct the \nsurvey. Once the survey is done  and it is seen that the line does \nnot disturb the wildlife and is environmentally feasible, then \nfurther action with regard to formation of the Special Purpose \nVehicle (SPV) can be taken.” \n (b) and (c): Meeting of Chairman, Railway Board with Hon’ble Chie f \nMinister, Government of Kerala was held at Thiruvananthapuram on \n27.10.2017 and the issue regarding development of Thalassery -Mysore  \nnew broad gauge line was discussed. It was decided that Detailed \nProject Report (DPR) should be prepared and submitted t o State \nGovernment and Ministry of Railways by 31.12.2017. \n(d) and (e): No Madam. Kerala Rail Development Corporation Limited \n(KRDCL), a Joint Venture Company of Government of Kerala and \nMinistry of Railways have nominated “Konkan Railway Corporation \nLimited” to take up the work of preparation of feasibility study and \nDPR for this new rail line from Thalassery to Mysore. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_78": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.78\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.12.2017\nVISIT OF PRIME MINISTER TO USA\n*78. COL. SONARAM CHOUDHARY:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the visit of the Prime Minister to USA recently with\nregard  to  defence  co-operation,  trade  etc.  along  with  the  other\nsubjects of mutual interest that were discussed; \n(b) whether during Indo-US talks, the issue of terrorism was discussed\nand if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the issue of grant of H1B visa to Indian IT professionals was\nalso discussed; and\n(d) if so, the details and the outcome thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER FOR EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK\nSABHA STARRED  QUESTION  NO.  78  REGARDING  “VISIT  OF PRIME\nMINISTER TO USA” FOR ANSWER ON 20.12.2017.\n(a) & (b) Prime Minister paid an official visit to the US from 25-26 June 2017 at\nthe  invitation  of  the  US  President  Donald  Trump.  Discussions  between  the  two\nleaders covered all key areas of India-US Strategic Partnership: defence and security;\ncounter-terrorism; trade and economic issues; energy; people-to-people contacts; and\nregional and international issues. \nKey outcomes and understanding reached were reflected in the Joint Statement\nissued during the visit and are summarized below:\n The leaders agreed to deepen defense and maritime-security cooperation. The\ntwo countries agreed to work together on advanced defense equipment and\ntechnology at a level commensurate with that of the closest allies and partners\nof the United States. \n The leaders agreed to strengthen the India-US partnership in the Indo-Pacific\nregion, and committed to a set of common principles to ensure peace and\nprosperity in the region.\n The leaders committed to intensifying their economic cooperation; removing\nobstacles to growth and jobs creation; and pursuing increased commercial\nengagement in a manner that advances the principles of free and fair trade.\nThe two countries will undertake a comprehensive review of trade relations\nwith the goal of expediting regulatory processes; ensuring that technology and\ninnovation are appropriately fostered, valued, and protected; and increasing\nmarket access in goods and services.\n The Leaders committed to strengthen counter-terrorism cooperation including\nby expanding information-sharing arrangements. It was decided to establish a\nnew  consultation  mechanism  on  domestic  and  international  terrorist\ndesignations  listing  proposals.  The  US  decided  to  designate  Hizb-ul-\nMujahideen  leader  Syed  Salahuddin  as  a  Specially  Designated  Global\n\nTerrorist. The leaders also affirmed their support for a U.N. Comprehensive\nConvention on International Terrorism. \n The leaders called on Pakistan to ensure that its territory is not used to launch\nterrorist  attacks  on  other  countries.  They  further  called  on  Pakistan  to\nexpeditiously bring to justice the perpetrators of the 26/11 Mumbai, Pathankot,\nand other cross-border terrorist attacks perpetrated by Pakistan-based groups.\n The U.S. expressed strong support for India’s early membership of multilateral\nexport  control  regimes  and  reaffirmed  the  support  for  India’s  permanent\nmembership of a reformed UN Security Council.\n(c) & (d) The issues of market access in services and fostering of technology and\ninnovation, in which H-1B visa is one element, was discussed in detail during the\nmeetings of Prime Minister’s visit to U.S. In our engagements, we emphasized that\nIndian skilled professionals have contributed to the growth and development of the\nU.S. economy and have helped the U.S. retain its competitive edge and innovation\nadvantage. They are a big stakeholder in India-U.S. relations.\nIn Prime Minister’s meeting with President Trump, the two leaders discussed\nfostering innovation, entrepreneurship and digital partnership. The leaders agreed to\nintensify the mutually beneficial digital partnership to fully harness their innovation\ncapabilities. Pursuant to the decision reached during the visit, India and the U.S. co-\nhosted the Global Entrepreneurship Summit.\nSo  far,  no  comprehensive  changes  have  been  made  to  the  H-1B  visa\nprogramme.  Government  of  India  remains  closely  engaged  with  the  U.S.\nAdministration and the U.S. Congress on issues relating to mobility of Indian skilled\nprofessionals, including through H-1B visa programme.\n*****",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_79": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.79 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.12.2017 \n  \nScarcity of Coal \n  \n*79 : SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA: \n  \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether there is a scarcity of coal in the country, if so, the details thereof and \nthe reasons therefor; \n  \n(b) whether the production of power in power plants has been affected as a result \nthereof and if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(c) whether the State of Karnataka has sought more coal for its power plants; and \n  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF COAL IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL AND RAILWAYS \n  \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n  \nA statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo.79 for answer on 20.12.2017 asked by Shri R. Dhurva Narayana. \n  \n(a) & (b):  Primarily due to failure on the part of the power houses to stoc k adequate \ncoal, as per Central Electricity Authority (CEA)  guidelines, during the lean power \ngeneration period and unexpected spurt in demand for thermal power arising out of \ndrop in power generation from other sources, like nuclear, hydro and wind energy  \nduring the 2nd quarter of 2017 -18 there was a drop in the stock position with the \nthermal power plants. Abnormally heavy monsoon in mine areas affected production, \nloading at mines and transportation of coal. With increased coal despatch from Coal \nIndia L imited (CIL)  sources and continuous monitoring, the situation of coal stock \nimproved at the Power Houses. The despatch from CIL during the period April to \nDecember, 2017 grew by about 9% as compared to despatch during the same \nperiod of 2016 -17. This incre ased coal supply has been mainly responsible for the \n4.7% increase in coal based power generation. This increased domestic coal based \npower generation coexists with 4.59% reduction in power generation by imported \ncoal based power plants of private sector. \n  \n(c) & (d ):  The CIL has pro rata  linkage of 3.356 MT and Singareni Collieries \nCompany Limited (SCCL) has pro rata linkage of 2.078 MT for supply of coal to the \nRaichur power plant of State of Karnataka. The materialization of the pro rata annual \ncontracted quantity from CIL and SCCL for 2017 -18 till 13 th December 2017 is \naround 77% and 100% respectively. It  is set to rise further in the remaining period of \nthis fiscal. The coal supply against bridge linkage from SCCL is on a best effort \nbasis. On the request of the State Government, the duration of Bridge Linkage to \nBellary Thermal Power Station (TPS) and Yeramarus TPS from SCCL has been \nextended twice. \n  \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_8": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n***** \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*8 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY THE 15TH DECEMBER, 2017 \nAGRAHAYANA 24, 1939 (SAKA) \n \n \nWAIVER/DEFERMENT OF GST \n \n*8. SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has received any proposal from the State \nGovernment of Telangana requesting to waive/defer the Goods and Services Tax \n(GST) on agricultural, drinking water, housing and highway projects for  a period of \ntwo years; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Union Government has taken any decision on the request of the \nState Government of Telangana; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor along with the stand of  \nthe Union Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FINANCE \n       SHRI ARUN JAITLEY \n \n(a), (b), (c), and (d): A statement is placed on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.8 RAISED BY SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY FOR 15 TH \nDECEMBER, 2017 REGARDING WAIVER/ DEFERMENT OF GST \n \nPart (a) and (b):  There is no GST on agriculture.  Regarding GST on works of \ndrinking water, housing and highway projects, some States including Telangana had \nrequested f or a change in the duty structure which was placed before the GST \nCouncil. \nPart (c) and (d):  The decisions regarding GST rates are taken by GST Council after \ndue deliberations.  The GST Council has already taken a decision to reduce GST on \ngovernment work s contract at 12% with full input tax credit and for government \nworks contract involving material component less than 25% of the contract value, the \nGST rate is reduced to 5%. \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_80": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.80 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.12.2017 \n \nIRREGULARITIES IN CATERING CONTRACTS \n  \n†*80. SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH:  \n          SHRIMATI REKHA VERMA:      \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether cases of fraud/irregularities have come to the notice and \ncomplaints have also been received in the name of granting extension in \nthe catering contracts in various States of the country including Madhya \nPradesh; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the action taken in this regard, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has done any evaluation of the loss of \nrevenue caused to the railways each year due to the said scam; if so, \nthe details including estimated loss as a  result thereof, the reaction of \nthe Government thereto and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(d) whether the Government has also received complaints against \nprivate catering firms/companies/agencies regarding charging of higher \nprices over and above the pres cribed prices of various items and the \nviolation of guidelines/approved contracts during the last three years; \nand  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof including the names of the catering \nfirms/agencies involved in such irregularities against whom investigation \nhas been undertaken and the outcome thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COAL \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 80 BY SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH AND SHRIMATI REKHA \nVERMA TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.12.2017 REGARDING \nIRREGULARITIES IN CATERING CONTRACTS \n \n(a) : No, Madam.  \n(b) : Does not arise.  \n(c) : There is no loss of revenue to the Railways.  \n(d): In its endeavour to provide quality and hygienic food to the passengers, \nRailways have developed and operationalized an institutionalized mechanism \nfor monitoring of quality and hygiene of catering services through regular \ninspections at various level s to address catering complaints. Year -wise total \nnumber of complaints including  those against private catering \nfirms/companies /Agencies regarding charging of Higher Prices of various \nitems and the violation of guidelines/approved contracts , received by the \nRailways for charging more than the prescribed prices and violation of \nguidelines during the last three years, year-wise are as under:-  \nHead \nYear wise No. of Complaints related to catering services \nover Indian Railways \n        2014-15            2015-16         2016 -17   2017-18       \n(01.04.2017 to          \n31.10.2017)   \n(7 Months) \nOver Charging  1403 2809 4156 2890 \nQuality  3249 3032 2464 1059 \nQuantity 404 237 243 117 \nMisbehaviour 215 216 217 137 \nHygiene 116 158 302 138 \nMiscellaneous 1668 2256 3055 1324 \nTotal 7055 8708 10437 5665 \n \nContd..2/- \n\n..2.. \nHead \nYear wise Action Taken on Complaints related to catering \nservices over Indian Railways \n        2014-15            2015-16         2016 -17          2017-18       \n(01.04.2017 to          \n31.10.2017) \nFined  2829 3568 3882 2505 \nWarned 2438 2684 2288 1305 \nTermination 3 2 2 12 \nSuitably \nAdvised 638 938 649 440 \nNot \nSubstantiated 465 664 532 394 \nD & AR Action 108 15 21 4 \nAny Other 574 837 1113 1005 \nTotal 7055 8708 10437 5665 \n \n \n(e): No, Madam. No investigation is going on. Suitable penal action has been \ninitiated as per extant provisions depending on the gravity of the lapse. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_81": "OIH \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.81 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15.12.2017 \n \nDischarge of Polluted Water into Rivers \n \n81. SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the discharge of poisonous and polluted \nwastewater by distillery unit of Riga sugar mill in Sitamarhi district of Bihar into \nManusmara river, thereby, destroying the fertility of thousands of acres of land and \nlives of human beings and domestic animals;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto; \n(c) the efforts made by the Government to make Manusmara river pollution free; and  \n(d) the success achieved by the Government as a result thereof so far? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (d)   Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) has inspected the distillery unit of M/s \nRiga Sugar Mill several times and has issued necessary directions to check pollution from the \nunit. In compliance of the directions issued by CPCB, the unit has discontinued diversion of \nspent wash to the Manusmara River; leakage of spent wash into press mud stored in open \nfield has been arrested and the field has been converted into banana plantation; the \ncontaminated press mud has been shifted to the press mud storage area in the bio composting \nyard; excess storage capacity of lagoon has been nullified by providing red colored marking \nand measuring scale at the lagoon; practice of land application of spent wash has been \ndiscontinued and the tankers used for the same has been dismantled. The unit has also \ninstalled Bio-digester, Reverse Osmosis (RO) and Multi Effect Evaporator (MEE) and has \nprovided connectivity of Online Continuous Effluent Monitoring System (OCEMS) to CPCB \nserver. Further, the analysis of Manusmara river water samples from 9 locations and ground \nwater samples from 7 locations does not indicate any contamination from industrial \ndischarge. \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_82": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 82\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15TH DECEMBER, 2017\nPICTORIAL WARNING ON TOBACCO PRODUCTS\n82. SHRI ANOOP MISHRA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the initiatives taken by this Ministry under the National Tobacco Control\nProgramme (NTCP) guidelines to ensure that all products manufactured in registered factories\nprint the pictorial health warning;\n(b) the details of the monitoring mechanism implemented for the same and details of the cases\nbeing reported for noncompliance and action taken by the department since April 2016 till now,\nState-wise;\n(c) the details of the Bidi Rollers who have been sensitized about the health hazards of bidi\nrolling in coordination with Health Department during the three years, State-wise; and\n(d) the details of implementation of vocational training of bidi rollers for alternative livelihood\nopportunities and the number of bidi rollers benefited by the same during the said period, State -\nwise?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL)\n(a) & (b): The Mini stry communicates to several Central Ministries/Departments viz.\nRevenue (Central Board of Excise and Customs), Civil Aviation, Tourism, Labour, External\nAffairs, Commerce, etc. and States/Union Territories about the notification of new health\nwarning as a nd when it is notified and also provides soft copies of notified specified health\nwarning to them for its compliance.\nA provision has been made in National Tobacco Control Programme (NTCP) guidelines\nthat Secretary Labour/Labour Commissioner or the Nominee (member of the State Level\nCoordination Committee), Sales Tax Officer & District Labour Officer (member of the District\nLevel Coordination Committee) shall ensure that all tobacco products manufactured in registered\nfactories print the pictorial health warning.\n\nThe onus of enforcing various provisions of the Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products\n(Prohibition of Advertisement and Regulation of Trade and Commerce, Production, Supply and\nDistribution) Act, 2003 (COTPA, 2003), lies with the States/UTs.\nThe details of the cases reported for non -compliance of the same and action taken in this\nregard since April 2016 till now are enclosed at Annexure – I.\n(c) & (d): The Ministry of Labour and Employment has initiated a Skill Development\nProgramme this year in coordination with this Ministry, World Health Organization,\nInternational Labour Organization, United Nations Development Programme and other\nMinistries for beed i workers. Through this programme, beedi rollers are also being sensitized\nabout the health hazards of bidi rolling. Details of the beneficiaries under this programme are at\nAnnexure-I1.\n\nAnnexure-I\n* These figures are for the calendar Year 2016 and 2017 respectively.\nS\n.\nN\n.\nStates/UTs 2016-17 2017-18\n(till date)\nNo. of\ncases\nAction\ntaken\nAmount\ncollected\n(in Rs.)\nNo. of\ncases\nAction\ntaken\nAmount\ncollected\n(in Rs.)\n1. Assam 47 Fined 15,750 13 Fined 11,700\n2. Gujarat 190 Challan\nissued\n26,100 51 Challan\nissued\n5,300\n3. Jharkhand - - - 3 FIR lodged,\ninvestigatio\nn underway\n-\n4. Karnataka 27 - - 106 - -\n5. Kerala* 26 - 0 248 - 0\n6. Odisha 1 Forwarded\nto court\n- - - -\n7. Punjab 1 Seized loose\ncigarettes\ncontaining\nnicotine.\nCase\nlaunched in\nthe court of\nCJM\nJalandhar\nunder\nsection\n22/5/7 of\nCOTPA\n2003 (State\nVs. Manoj\nPuri (2015)\nat CJM\nJalandhar)\n1,000 62 Challans\nissued by\nthe Deptt. of\nLegal\nMetrology\nagainst\nloose\ncigarettes\n(25 challans\npaid and 37\npending)\n97,500\n8. Rajasthan 31\nseizure\n22\npresented\nin court\n- 4 seizure 3 presented\nin court and\n1 penalized\n2,000\n9. Uttar Pradesh 933 Yes 9,61,500 1,312 Yes 11,51,200\n\nAnnexure-II\nSTATUS OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT OF BE